1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,150
Two pesos.

2
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:05,200
In the Philippines, two pesos could send a text message across the country.

3
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:10,450
And that tiny tingi trick turned Filipinos into the texting capital of the world.

4
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:14,500
Here’s how Filipino engineers hacked the telecom system to make it happen.

5
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:16,300
Manila, 1999.

6
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:18,550
Back then, cellphones were only for the rich.

7
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:21,850
If you wanted prepaid load, you had to buy a plastic scratch card.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,400
The cheapest one? 300 pesos. Sometimes 500.

9
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:27,350
Isang araw na sweldo na ’yan.

10
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:31,400
So at neighborhood sari-sari stores, people kept asking the same question:

11
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:34,800
"Ate, wala bang tingi? Kahit pang-isang text lang?"

12
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,900
At the time, telecom giants thought that was impossible.

13
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:42,100
Printing millions of two-peso plastic cards would cost more than the load itself.

14
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:45,600
Pero may napansin ang mga Filipino engineers.

15
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,200
Look at how a cell tower actually works.

16
00:00:48,350 --> 00:00:53,150
When you make a voice call, your phone opens a dedicated radio voice channel. That takes bandwidth.

17
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:55,100
Kaya mahal ang tawag.

18
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:59,900
But even when your phone is idle in your pocket, it constantly talks to the nearest cell tower.

19
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:05,800
Every few seconds, they exchange tiny status pings over a hidden lane called the GSM control channel.

20
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,800
And inside every control packet, there were 160 bytes of empty space, just sitting there, free.

21
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:14,950
That’s where SMS rides.

22
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:21,650
One text message is just 160 characters hitching a free ride on a signal the tower was already sending anyway.

23
00:01:21,850 --> 00:01:24,800
Zero extra radio lines. Almost zero cost.

24
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:29,300
So in 2003, engineers at Smart asked a simple question:

25
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:35,300
If Filipinos buy shampoo, coffee, and toyo in tiny sachets, why not telecom load?

26
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:37,900
They threw away the plastic scratch card.

27
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:41,950
Instead, they built the world’s first Over-The-Air electronic loading system.

28
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:50,500
Using a special encrypted SIM card, any sari-sari store owner could slice airtime into tiny digital sachets and beam it straight to a customer’s phone.

29
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:53,900
Then they went one step further. They launched PasaLoad.

30
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:57,300
Just type a number, type two pesos, and hit send.

31
00:01:57,450 --> 00:02:03,150
In two seconds, the network’s central ledger subtracts two pesos from your balance and credits your friend’s phone.

32
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:06,900
Walang bangko. Walang card. Load lang, passed from phone to phone.

33
00:02:07,150 --> 00:02:12,250
The result was explosive. Overnight, millions of ordinary Filipinos got connected.

34
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:19,900
By the late 2000s, the Philippines was sending 1.3 billion text messages every single day—more than the US and Europe combined.

35
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:24,650
In London, the Filipino engineering team won Best Mobile Innovation in the World.

36
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:31,100
And soon, telcos in over 20 countries across Asia and Africa copied the exact same micro-prepaid architecture.

37
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:38,850
Even better? That simple SMS ledger became the blueprint for the world’s first mobile wallets, long before Apple Pay or QR codes existed.

38
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:42,850
All because someone at a sari-sari store asked for tingi.

39
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:48,450
Ikaw, naabutan mo pa ba ang PasaLoad na dalawang piso, o nakapag-scratch ka pa ng 300 pesos na card?
